On the inverse limit $D_\infty = \mathrm{InverseLimit}\,D\,P$, the coordinatewise order [Order on the inverse limit](1972_inverseLimitLE.md) **`inverseLimitLE`** is the natural $\le$. The **strict** comparison is the usual one for a partial order: below in $\le$ but not above.

**Definition (`inverseLimitLT`).** For **`x`**, **`y : InverseLimit D P`**,

$$\texttt{inverseLimitLT}\,D\,P\,x\,y \;\;\Longleftrightarrow\;\;
\texttt{inverseLimitLE}\,D\,P\,x\,y \;\wedge\; \neg\,\texttt{inverseLimitLE}\,D\,P\,y\,x.$$

Equivalently: $x \le y$ coordinatewise and it is **not** the case that $y \le x$ coordinatewise (so some coordinate is strictly below, or the sequences differ).

In the namespace **`inverseLimitLT`** we have

**Theorem (`iff_LE_not_GE`).** *For all **`x`**, **`y`**,*

$$\texttt{inverseLimitLT}\,D\,P\,x\,y \;\;\Longleftrightarrow\;\;
\texttt{inverseLimitLE}\,D\,P\,x\,y \;\wedge\; \neg\,\texttt{inverseLimitLE}\,D\,P\,y\,x.$$

*Proof.* Definition of **`inverseLimitLT`**. $\square$
